---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/base-layout.astro';
import EngagementTimeline from '../components/engagement-timeline.astro';
import SectionNav from '../components/section-nav.astro';
import CommandPalette from '../components/command-palette.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const sections = [
  { id: 'methodology', label: 'Methodology' },
  { id: 'timeline', label: 'The ten steps' },
  { id: 'safety', label: 'Safety controls' },
  { id: 'excluded', label: 'Excluded techniques' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'Your data' },
  { id: 'independence', label: 'Independence' },
  { id: 'stop', label: 'Stopping the test' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How we work"
  description="Methodology, engagement timeline, safety controls, excluded techniques and data handling. What actually happens during an Attestor Security assessment."
  path="/how-we-work"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="page">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1>What happens, in the order it happens</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Testing a production system is a controlled activity with legal weight behind it. This page
        is the whole procedure, including the parts that constrain us.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="page split">
      <SectionNav sections={sections} />

      <div class="body">
        <section id="methodology">
          <h2>Methodology</h2>
          <p>
            We follow published standards rather than a house method, because your auditor and your
            buyer's security team can check a published standard and cannot check ours.
          </p>
          <ul class="rule-list">
            {
              site.methodology.map((entry) => (
                <li>
                  <strong>{entry.name}</strong>
                  {entry.version ? ` ${entry.version}` : ''}
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
          <p>
            Coverage is recorded against those standards as testing runs, and the report contains a
            matrix marking every category as tested, partially tested or not tested, with a reason
            in every case where it is not fully tested. That matrix is generated from what actually
            executed. It is the section auditors read first and it is the section that protects both
            of us when something is missed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/what-we-test">The complete check catalogue is published</a>, with the standard
            identifier each check maps to.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="timeline">
          <EngagementTimeline />
        </section>

        <section id="safety">
          <h2>Safety controls</h2>
          <p>
            These are enforced by the platform, not by anyone's discipline. Each one is a code path
            that refuses rather than a policy somebody remembers.
          </p>
          <ul class="rule-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Nothing runs without a signed authorisation.</strong> Every tool launch checks
              that an authorisation exists for the engagement, is signed, is not revoked, and that
              the current time is inside its validity window. There is one code path that starts a
              tool and it makes that check first.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Scope is checked after DNS resolution, not before.</strong> A hostname you own
              can resolve to shared infrastructure somebody else owns. The resolved addresses are
              checked against scope too, and a mismatch stops the run and asks a human.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Crawling cannot walk out of scope.</strong> Redirects and discovered links are
              re-checked before they are followed.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Rate limits are enforced in code.</strong> Global and per-target requests per
              second, concurrency caps, and a polite mode that halves everything for production
              targets. Configuration can lower these limits and cannot raise them past the ceiling.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Adaptive abort.</strong> Target latency and error rate are watched throughout.
              Degradation backs the testing off automatically; further degradation aborts the run and
              alerts the tester.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Dry run first.</strong> Every new engagement is run in dry-run mode, which
              shows exactly which targets and which checks would run without sending a packet.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Read-only first pass.</strong> On production systems the first pass suppresses
              every state-changing request.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Dangerous actions are excluded from crawling.</strong> Logout, delete account,
              change password, change email, payment submission, mass delete and invite-user are on a
              default exclusion list, and you can extend it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A stop button that works.</strong> One control halts every running job for your
              engagement within seconds. Who pressed it and when is recorded.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="excluded">
          <h2>Techniques we exclude</h2>
          <p>
            These are not available on request. Some are excluded because they are prohibited by
            cloud providers, some because they cause harm without producing evidence, and some
            because they need a separate written agreement we are not going to fold into a testing
            contract.
          </p>
          <ul class="rule-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Denial of service, in any form.</strong> No stress testing, no load testing, no
              volumetric testing, no simulated DDoS. AWS and Azure prohibit it outright. There is no
              flag in our platform that enables it, because we did not build one.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Destructive payloads.</strong> No statement that deletes or modifies data in
              bulk, no file deletion, no account deletion, no password changes on real accounts, no
              shell persistence.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Mass data extraction.</strong> Injection findings are confirmed on the schema
              and on a minimal masked sample. We do not extract your customer table to prove a point.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Social engineering and phishing of your staff.</strong> Possible as a separate
              engagement with its own written sign-off. Never bundled in.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Physical intrusion.</strong> Not a service we offer.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Anything outside the signed asset list.</strong> Including as a favour,
              including if you ask nicely mid-engagement. A new asset is a new authorisation.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="data">
          <h2>Your data and your credentials</h2>
          <ul class="rule-list">
            <li>
              <strong>Credentials arrive through a one-time link</strong> into an encrypted vault.
              Not by email, not by chat, not in a ticket. They carry an expiry and a role name, and
              they are decrypted only inside the process that uses them, in memory, at run time.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Personal data is masked as evidence is captured</strong>, not when the report
              is written. Email addresses, phone numbers, identity numbers and card numbers are
              masked before anything reaches disk, and you can add patterns specific to your data.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nothing that could be a secret reaches a log.</strong> Every log sink, every
              stored tool output and every error trace passes through a redaction filter first.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Evidence is deleted {site.evidenceRetentionDays} days after report release</strong>,
              and you receive a written deletion confirmation naming what was destroyed. We keep the
              report, the signed engagement documents and the activity log, because those are our
              defence if anything is ever disputed.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>On closure your credentials are cryptographically destroyed</strong> and we
              tell you to rotate the accounts anyway.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>We act as a data processor under the DPDP Act</strong> and will sign a data
              processing agreement. We bring one if you do not have your own.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nothing goes to a third-party AI service</strong> unless you have specifically
              enabled it for your engagement, and even then only redacted findings text — never raw
              evidence, never credentials, never customer data.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="independence">
          <h2>Independence</h2>
          <p>
            {site.brandName} does not design, build, operate or maintain the systems it assesses, and
            holds no financial or operational interest in its clients beyond the fee for the
            engagement. That is what makes the report usable as third-party evidence — ISO 27001
            competence expectations and audit independence norms both depend on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also means we will not fix the code we audited. If you want remediation help, we will
            introduce you to someone; we will not audit our own work and then attest to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            {site.brandName} is not empanelled by CERT-In and does not claim any certification it
            does not hold. If an assessment needs an empanelled organisation, we will tell you on the
            first call.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="stop">
          <h2>Stopping the test</h2>
          <p>
            Before testing starts you nominate a contact with the authority to halt it, reachable
            throughout the window. One message stops everything. There is no approval chain on our
            side and no argument about whether the concern is justified — we stop, then we talk.
          </p>
          <p>
            If we find something critical, you hear about it as soon as it is validated, with the
            reproduction steps, ahead of the report and by phone if that is faster. Waiting until
            report day to mention a critical finding is a practice that exists to make reports look
            impressive, and it is indefensible.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CommandPalette />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  h1 {
    max-width: 18ch;
  }

  .body > section {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    scroll-margin-top: 5.5rem;
  }

  .body h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
    padding-top: var(--space-s);
  }
</style>
